    "result_text": "All 22 tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature: on owner creation, the API now records the number of existing owners sharing the same first name and last name (case-insensitively) *before* this create, and returns it on subsequent reads.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a read-only `namesakeCount` (int32, min 0) property to the `Owner` schema, which regenerates `OwnerDto` with the field (MapStruct auto-maps it from the model getter).\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a persisted `namesake_count` column (`Integer namesakeCount`) with getter/setter, so the value survives the create and is returned on `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Before persisting, sets `namesakeCount` via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper that counts existing owners matching both names using the existing `normalizeForComparison` (trim + collapse whitespace + lowercase), so the comparison is case-insensitive and the not-yet-saved owner is excluded.\n\n4. **Schema DDL** \u2014 Added `namesake_count INTEGER` to all four `owners` tables (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres); hsqldb is what the tests use.\n\nThe new `Cp13Tests.coreCountsNamesakes` passes (two owners named \"Ann Sur\u2026\" \u2192 the second reads back `namesakeCount = 1`), and all pre-existing acceptance tests remain green.",
